# Line endings, decided here rather than by each machine's core.autocrlf.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout can show files as modified with an empty
# `git diff` — the content is identical, only the line endings differ — and
# `git checkout <branch>` then refuses to switch. That cost a build: the
# checkout aborted, the pull never happened, and the resulting installer was
# silently built from stale code while looking like a normal build.

# Normalise to LF in the repository, and let Git hand the working tree whatever
# the platform expects.
* text=auto

# Scripts that must keep LF wherever they are checked out. A shell script with
# CRLF fails with a confusing "bad interpreter" error.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell tolerates LF, but it is a Windows-first file and CRLF is what an
# editor there will produce anyway — so say so instead of leaving it to chance.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary, so Git neither diffs nor converts them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.wav binary
*.bin binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
